The !!Rosetta Stone decree!!, or the !!Decree of Memphis!!, is the [[ Ptolemaic decree | ptolemaic-synodal-decrees ]] that enabled the [[ decipherment of Egyptian hieroglyphs | decipherment-of-ancient-egyptian-scripts ]], and it did so because it was set down in three scripts and two languages: [[ Egyptian hieroglyphs ]], [[ Egyptian Demotic | demotic-egyptian-script ]] and [[ Ancient Greek ]]. What it says was decided by a council of priests at [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]] in 196 BC, confirming the royal cult of [[ Ptolemy V ]].

The decree also mentions the Egyptian rebellion against the Greek rulers, which is otherwise known only through Greek sources and remains of graffiti.[[ ^1 ]]

== Context

Ptolemy V Epiphanes became ruler at the age of five, after the sudden death of both his parents. [[ Ptolemy IV Philopator ]] and his wife and sister Arsinoe were murdered in a conspiracy that involved Ptolemy IV's mistress [[ Agathoclea | agathoclea-mistress ]], according to contemporary sources, and the conspirators then effectively ruled Egypt as the boy's guardians[[ ^6 ]][[ ^5 ]] until a revolt broke out two years later under the general [[ Tlepolemus | tlepolemus-regent-of-egypt ]], when Agathoclea and her family were lynched by a mob in Alexandria. Tlepolemus was himself replaced as guardian in 201 BC by [[ Aristomenes of Alyzia | aristomenes-of-alyzeia ]], who was chief minister at the time of the Memphis decree.[[ ^7 ]] Ptolemy V reigned from 204 to 181 BC.

The kingdom he inherited was losing ground on both sides of its own borders. The native Egyptian population at home was treated as second-class citizens by its [[ Greek rulers | ptolemaic-dynasty ]] and imperialists, while the economy had suffered because of the wars.[[ ^1 ]] The [[ Seleucids | seleucid-empire ]] won over the previously Ptolemaic-ruled territory of [[ Coele-Syria ]], including [[ Judaea | judea ]], after the [[ Battle of Panium ]] (198 BC), and other territories were divided between [[ Antiochus III the Great ]] and [[ Philip V of Macedon ]], who had seized several islands and cities in [[ Caria ]] and [[ Thrace ]].

During the reign of Ptolemy IV,[[ ^2 ]] the long-standing [[ Great Theban Revolt ]] (206–186 BC)[[ ^3 ]] had begun in the south of Egypt, led by [[ Horwennefer ]] and by his successor [[ Ankhwennefer | ankhwennefer-pharaoh ]].[[ ^4 ]] It was still going on when the young Ptolemy V was officially crowned at Memphis at the age of 12, seven years after the start of his reign.[[ ^5 ]] Egyptian cities that joined the rebellion had faced economic and political consequences such as the closure of ports.[[ ^1 ]]

The coronation came in the year after Ptolemy V's [[ majority | age-of-majority ]], and it was for that occasion that the priests of Egypt assembled at Memphis and passed the decree.[[ ^8 ]]

== Text

The text as recorded on the Rosetta Stone is considered the most complete of any of the surviving stelae, as it preserves the inscription in three scripts and two languages.[[ ^9 ]] The first script is Egyptian hieroglyphs, the second is [[ Demotic | demotic-egyptian-script ]], and the third is Greek capitals. Complete is relative. Only parts of the last fourteen lines of hieroglyphs remain, and these correspond to the last twenty-eight lines of Greek text, which is also damaged. The Demotic section is thirty-two lines of which the starts of fourteen are damaged (reading right to left). Fifty-four lines of Greek are present, twenty-six of which are damaged at the ends.[[ ^10 ]] The majority of the damaged and missing sections of the hieroglyphic portion can be restored using a copy of the decree from Damanhur ([[ Hermopolis Magna | hermopolis ]]) which was discovered in 1896. It post-dates the Rosetta Stone by fourteen years and as such has slightly different content, omitting details that are no longer relevant.[[ ^10 ]]

Demotic was possibly the draft language of the decree, on the evidence of the section describing the shrine housing the statue of the king. The wording makes the most sense in Demotic; the Greek version does not make much sense, and the hieroglyphic version lacks a determinative, rendering the meaning unspecific.[[ ^11 ]] The text is a composite, however, with sections drawing on Greek or pharaonic traditions more than others.[[ ^12 ]]

The inscription opens with the date of the decree: "[Year 9, [[ Xandikos | ancient-macedonian-calendar ]], day] 4 which corresponds to the second month of the Egyptians, [[ Winter | season-of-the-emergence ]], day 18 (under) Pharaoh..."[[ ^13 ]] That corresponds to 27 March 196 B.C.[[ ^14 ]] It does not record the day the decree was issued, but the date on which priests assembled as part of national festivals. On the Rosetta Stone this is the coronation of the king; on other stelae the occasion is the installation of a sacred animal.[[ ^15 ]]

Much of what follows is a ledger of what the king had given away. The text records that Ptolemy reduced or abolished taxes for the army and general population: "Of the dues and taxes existing in Egypt some he has cut and others he has abolished completely in order to cause the army and all other people to be happy in his time as [Pharaoh]."[[ ^16 ]] Taxes levied on temples and their estates were also relaxed, with the order that "the divine revenues of the gods and the silver and grain which are given as syntaxis to their [temples] each year and the portions which accrue to the gods from vineyards and gardens and all other property over which they had rights under his father, that they remain in their possession..."[[ ^17 ]] These tax breaks extended to the priesthood, who were ordered "not to pay their tax for serving as priest above the amount they paid up to year one of his father. He has relieved the people [who are in] the offices of the temples of the sailing they make to [[ Alexandria ]] every year..."[[ ^17 ]]

The generosity of the king is also extolled: he granted amnesty to prisoners and outlawed [[ pressganging | impressment ]].[[ ^18 ]] Much space is dedicated to detailing the silver and grain given to temples, especially those centered on the animal cults, with Ptolemy making "numerous benefactions to the [[ Apis | apis-deity ]] and [[ Mnevis ]] and the other animals which are sacred in Egypt... his heart being concerned with their affairs at all times, giving whatever was desired for their burials great and revered and bearing that which occurred for them (at) their temples when they celebrate festivals and make burnt offerings before them and the other things it is fitting to do."[[ ^19 ]] Restoration and rebuilding of temples was carried out throughout Egypt as well.[[ ^19 ]]

Earlier copies of the text recount the king's victory in the siege of Shekan ([[ Lycopolis | lycopolis-delta ]]), which was "fortified by the enemy with every device."[[ ^20 ]] He shows amnesty to "the men who had been on the other side in the rebellion which occurred in Egypt, to let them [return] to their homes and their property belong to them (again)."[[ ^20 ]] The leaders of the rebellion are shown no such clemency. They are "slain at the stake" in Memphis as part of the festivities surrounding Ptolemy's ascension.[[ ^20 ]] Later versions from Upper Egypt refer to the conquering of Thebes after its rebellion.[[ ^21 ]]

What the priests give in return is the cult of the king. Temples throughout Egypt are ordered to "...set up a statue of Pharaoh Ptolemy... in the (most) conspicuous place in the temple..."[[ ^22 ]] The cult statue is to be made in Egyptian style, and is to be attended by priests three times daily, giving the image the same rituals they would perform for other gods.[[ ^22 ]] The priests of all temples throughout Egypt are given the title "priests of the God who appears, whose goodness is perfect" in addition to their existing titles.[[ ^23 ]] Even the shrine in which the statue is to be kept is specified: royal crowns replace the traditional [[ uraeus ]] frieze atop it, the centre-most crown being the [[ Double Crown | pschent ]], while the [[ papyrus | cyperus-papyrus ]] and [[ sedge | cyperaceae ]] are to appear on the corners, supporting a uraeus seated on a basket, "signifying Pharaoh who illumines [[ Upper and Lower Egypt ]]."[[ ^24 ]] The shrine is to be "...set it in the sanctuary with the other gold shrines."[[ ^22 ]]

The feast days of the king are assigned too. The "last day of the fourth month of Summer" is celebrated as the pharaoh's birthday, and the "seventeenth day of the second month of Winter" is to be celebrated as the anniversary of his ascension.[[ ^25 ]] The two days "...are to be celebrated as festival every month in all the temples of Egypt..."[[ ^25 ]] and, in addition, "they are to celebrate festival and procession in the temples and all of Egypt for Pharaoh Ptolemy... each year on the first day of the first month of Inundation for five days..."[[ ^25 ]] It is also stipulated that the statue of the king should take part in the major festivals of other deities, and that his statue should process with them.[[ ^24 ]]

The worship of the king is extended to the general population as well, with "ordinary people who so wish" to have in their homes a gold shrine similar to that found in the temples containing a statue of the king "and to celebrate the festivals and processions described above each year..."[[ ^23 ]]

The text ends with the instruction that temples throughout Egypt are to erect a stela bearing a copy of the decree recorded in three scripts and two languages.[[ ^23 ]]

== Copies

That closing instruction is why the decree survives at all. Ten securely dated copies are known. The Rosetta Stone preserves the earliest and most complete copy, from year 9 of Ptolemy V's reign. Two copies of the text were inscribed on the wall of [[ Philae | philae-temple-complex ]] temple: one, known as Philensis II, dates to year 19, while the second, Philensis I, dates to year 21. The latest dated text is a year 23 stela from Asphynis. Duplicates of the various versions exist, with three copies of the Rosetta Stone text known from [[ Naucratis ]], Elephantine, and Noub Taha, and a further copy each known for the Philensis inscriptions and Asphynis. Three additional fragmentary copies exist, but they are not securely dated to Ptolemy's reign.[[ ^26 ]]

The content of the text is broadly the same across the various copies, with later versions omitting or adding details as relevant. These include adding Ptolemy's wife, [[ Cleopatra I | cleopatra-i-syra ]], to the royal cult after their marriage, and recording military expeditions. The specific details also vary according to the location of the stele: copies from [[ Upper Egypt ]] record the reconquering of Thebes in year 19 instead of the siege of Lycopolis.[[ ^26 ]]

